"executive_summary": "This study reports a modified silica-supported bimetallic Au-Fe catalyst (AuFeHS) for the partial oxidation of methane (POM) to methanol and acetic acid using hydrogen peroxide (H2O2) as a green oxidant. The central motivation is that methane is abundant and cheap, yet its activation is difficult because the C-H bond is non-polar and thermodynamics favor complete oxidation to CO2. Prior work has largely relied on high-pressure batch reactors, molecular oxygen oxidants, or single-atom catalysts that still suffer from low selectivity and harsh conditions. This work instead targets mild, commercially attractive conditions. In a batch reactor at 10 bar methane pressure, the optimized AuFeHS catalyst (0.23 wt% Au, 0.46 wt% Fe) achieved a methanol yield of 1666 mmol/gFe+Au with 95% selectivity, demonstrating strong synergy between Au and Fe. Introducing CO as a co-feed switched selectivity toward acetic acid, giving 3403 µmol/gcat within 1 h at 50°C and 20 bar. The most significant advance is the continuous flow process at atmospheric pressure, where AuFeHS produced 224 mmol/gFe+Au of methanol over 7 h, with a productivity of 422 µmol h⁻¹ gcat⁻¹, surpassing prior high-pressure H2O2 and O2 benchmarks. Isotope (CD4) labeling confirmed that the methyl group in methanol originated from methane rather than the support. Mechanistically, the authors attribute performance to low-valent Fe 3-δ+ sites that activate both CH4 and H2O2, stabilized by Au nanoparticles, together with Lewis acid sites and a hydrophobic Na-containing silica support that keeps peroxide near the active sites and facilitates methanol desorption. The paper concludes that bimetallic modulation of Fe species offers a promising route toward commercializing methane-to-oxygenates (M2O) technologies under benign conditions.",
